"""
# And/Or Operands Route Through lower_cond_expr, Not Raw lower_expr

Regression guard for a bug in `lower_binary_expr`'s `case And | Or:` arm in
`src/compiler/50.mir/_MirLoweringExpr/expr_dispatch.spl`: both operands of a
logical `and`/`or` are used purely as branch conditions, so they must be
lowered via `lower_cond_expr` -- the same condition-normalizing entry point
`if`/`while` use -- not the raw value dispatcher `lower_expr`.

`lower_cond_expr`'s own docstring
(`src/compiler/50.mir/mir_lowering_stmts.spl:1836-1837`) already ASSERTS that
`and`/`or` funnel their operands back through the condition path via their
own lowering -- but until this fix the arm called `lower_expr` directly, so
that invariant was false in the actual code.

Consequence: `RT_NIL` is the runtime's NON-ZERO nil sentinel (the integer
`3`), so a `.?`/bare-Optional operand of `and`/`or`, branched on via raw
`lower_expr` instead of the condition-normalizing `lower_cond_expr`, tested
truthy REGARDLESS of its actual nilness -- e.g. `(x == nil) and rhs()` or
`opt.? or fallback()` would take the wrong branch whenever the left operand's
nil-check outcome should have controlled short-circuiting. Same defect class
as `bare_optional_in_condition_position_wrong_branch_2026-08-01`, which fixed
`if`/`while` but left `and`/`or` behind.

This is a source-content guard, not a runtime execution spec: the fix lives
in the pure-Simple self-hosted compiler
(`src/compiler/50.mir/_MirLoweringExpr/expr_dispatch.spl`), which the
currently deployed `bin/simple` (a Rust bootstrap seed, see `bin/simple
--version`) does not execute -- the seed has its own independent Rust MIR
lowering and never reads this file's compiled behavior. A true end-to-end
runtime check requires a self-hosted binary built via full bootstrap (T3),
out of scope for an ordinary spec run. This spec instead asserts the exact
fix shape is present in source, which is real, sabotage-provable evidence
today (revert the fix -> this spec goes RED; restore it -> GREEN).
"""

extern fn rt_file_read_text(path: text) -> text

describe "And/Or logical operands in MIR lowering (lower_binary_expr)":
    it "routes both the left and right operand through lower_cond_expr, not lower_expr":
        val source = rt_file_read_text(
            "src/compiler/50.mir/_MirLoweringExpr/expr_dispatch.spl") ?? ""
        expect(source).to_contain("case And | Or:")
        expect(source).to_contain("val left_local = self.lower_cond_expr(left)")
        expect(source).to_contain("val right_local = self.lower_cond_expr(right)")
        # Sabotage guard: the old (buggy) call shape must NOT be the one that
        # actually feeds left_local/right_local for the And|Or arm. We can't
        # assert global absence of "lower_expr(left)" (it's legitimately used
        # elsewhere, e.g. the `case _:` binary-op fallthrough), so we instead
        # pin the exact fixed lines above as the ones present -- if this file
        # is reverted to `val left_local = self.lower_expr(left)` right after
        # `case And | Or:`, the two to_contain checks above fail.
